WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.640
Black Hills will build 564 megawatts of gas in Cheyenne for Google's data center. This is AI Chips &amp; Data Centres, episode 19. Power engineering reports the plan has announced, not yet a plant pouring power. The turbines would sit at the existing Cheyenne Prairie Generating Station and go in between 2027 and 2029. The build is priced at $1.8 billion. A non-regulated Black Hills affiliate would own the new units, not the Wyoming utility.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.460
Black Hills is a South Dakota holding company, and it would also run about 2.1 gigawatts of

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.780
machines it does not own.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.680
The project release suggests those third-party resources are turbines and fuel cells.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.920
They would sit on a private microgrid under Wyoming's large power contract service tariff.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.400
Black Hills would collect a management fee for dispatch, reliability, and grid coordination.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.580
Add reserve margin, leave out new transmission, and the company puts the whole mix at about

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:02.560
2.7 gigawatts. Google is the tenant that pays to be served. The contract is written to keep

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.440
stranded assets and early exit and surprise costs off existing customers. Google has already

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:15.020
advanced $399 million for long-lead gear. Black Hills expects to pay that advance back before

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:21.900
July 2027. The company projects about $150 million of net income from the project in 2030.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.680
Unleivered free cash flow through 2048 is put at $2.4 billion dollars after the generation

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.260
investment.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.580
An air quality permit for the Cheyenne Prairie expansion is already in hand.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.640
Sighting and transmission approvals are still being sought.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.640
Biggo Finance puts Samsung's high bandwidth memory share at 33%.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.160
The figure comes from a damming research interview published on October 6th.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.100
The conversation itself was September 19th.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:59.820
With a process engineer at SK Heinex's Future Technology Research Institute, the notes call it a personal view, not the company line, and the full transcript sits behind a paywall.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.360
That engineer said SK Heinex is stuck on hybrid bonding for a later HBM generation.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.380
Samsung in the same account is already sending customers samples that use the process.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:16.560
Neither company has announced this, and the write-up names no generation, no stack height, and no buyer.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.160
Separately, on June 18th, SK Heinex said it had handed major customers 12-layer

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.320
HBM4E samples. Those samples are 48 gigabytes and use the company's

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:33.440
existing advanced MRMUF package. SK Heinex said thermal resistance is 17%

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:39.240
better than HBM4. EE Times reports global foundries unveiled FDX fusion in

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.860
Dresden on October 9th. It is strained silicon fully depleted silicon on

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.720
insulator, aimed at physical AI rather than a data center GPU.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.400
Global Foundry says digital performance sits in the class of a 7 nanometer node, and

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.800
that it gets there without extreme ultraviolet light.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.720
Density is more than double the company's first FDX generation.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:06.220
The same flow is meant to hold RF, analog, mix signal, embedded non-volatile memory, in

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:07.960
memory computing and dense logic.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:14.020
Manfred Horseman, who runs the European Fabs, told EE Times that data center AI burns

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:15.980
power in a different class of problem.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:22.320
Physical AI in his account also needs sensors, actuators, communications, low power, and

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.940
a cost that a machine can carry.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.640
Global Foundry's calls that set stack.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.100
Sense, think, act, communicate.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.120
The development spend is roughly $1 billion over the coming years.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.780
About $250 million of that is the first tool set.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:43.220
The channel is a silicon film about 70 angstroms thick, which is 7 nanometers, sitting on buried

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:43.680
oxide.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.940
That film is thin enough to be fully depleted on its own.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.260
The same day, global foundries topped off Sprint, the Dresden expansion backed by the

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.340
European Chips Act.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.120
Sprint adds clean room, lab, and manufacturing space.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.900
Semiconductor Digest ran the same roadmap.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.980
Semiconductor Digest also reports that microchip has bought Virago technologies.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.320
Virago is a private Austin firm that sells radiation, hardened and radiation tolerant

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.460
microcontrollers and microprocessors.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:17.640
Microchip says the deal is closed and describes itself as a broadline chip supplier.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.120
Data Center Dynamics reports a council in Northern Italy has turned down an 80 MW data

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:23.520
center.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.640
The municipality says the plans cut against its environmental policy.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.060
Data Center Knowledge reports firmness and PLDT pull data center stock offerings this

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:33.280
week.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.800
government in the region also pressed pause on new development. HPC Wire reports

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.100
that on October 9th, 2026, Fermilab took two new awards under the Department of

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.700
Energy's Genesis mission. The work is custom microelectronics plus quantum

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.840
information science. The mission is described as a national push to double

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.780
productivity and overall impact. Take it apart at the module, not at the logo on

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.900
the box. A finished memory part does not record whether its die was bit

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:06.920
against an accelerator. The split happens at wafer start and again in the back end that

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:12.220
makes a die something a board can take. Stacked high band with parts eat advanced DRAM,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:18.880
bonding time and test time. Ordinary DRAM and NAN still need clean room hours, probe slots,

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.540
substrates and module lines from the same industry. When that shared capacity tightens, a line

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.720
that used to be small on a bill of materials becomes a line that sets the shelf price. Tech

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.720
Back power up, citing Sir Cana numbers, Matt Piscatella posted on Blue Sky, measured

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.980
US console hardware for 2026 through August.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.080
Xbox unit sales were down 33% from a year earlier.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:49.300
Buyers paid an average of $529 for a new Xbox, a new PlayStation average $597.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:56.100
August hardware sales were $559,000 units, the lowest August total since 2013.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.180
Piscatella tied the drop to price hikes that followed the memory and storage shortage.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:04.420
A fab books the wafer start long before the shelf date.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.900
A start aimed at a stacked DRAM die does not later become a console chip.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.780
A standard DRAM or NAND start still fights for testers, engineers, and often the same substrate

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.060
vendors the AI package is already loading.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.200
The device maker then hits a higher contract price and a ship slot that can slip after that

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:23.140
price is accepted.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:29.000
unit's fall when either one binds. The average price in that cercana cut is what is left after

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.900
the buyer who would not pay has already walked. The sticker stops being the lever that clears

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:39.560
the market when the module is simply not on the line. Testors full, substrates late, or nanned

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:45.480
held for data center drives, and a discount still does not create a unit. Availability is what then

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:51.960
decides whether a launch weekend ships. On the horizon, demonstrator silicon for FDX fusion is aimed

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.500
at early 2027, and the first process design kit at mid-2027.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:02.500
Black Hills expects microgrid management fees to start paying a return on the Cheyenne

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:03.560
project next year.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:08.760
The new gas units themselves are scheduled across 2027 through 2029.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:14.960
Before we go, the nearest date on that list is the FDX Fusion demonstrator in early 2027.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.720
That's AI Chips and Data Centers for today.

